# .fork/REGEN — "take theirs + regenerate + stage" rules for conflicted GENERATED files.
#
# port.sh already auto-resolves conflicted LOCKFILES this way (upstream's side, then
# the ecosystem's own re-resolver, then stage — a hand-merged lockfile only ever LOOKS
# right). This table extends the same recipe to YOUR project's other generated files
# (dumped DB schemas, generated API clients, compiled translations, ...).
#
# Format: one rule per non-comment line —
#
#     <path-glob><whitespace><command...>
#
# The first field is a shell glob (or literal path) matched against each conflicted
# path (same matching as the registry's Touches anchors); the REST of the line is the
# regeneration command, run via `bash -c` from the repo root. First matching rule wins.
#
# Contract, on a conflict matching a rule: port.sh takes UPSTREAM'S side of the file,
# then runs the command to re-express your fork's state on top (exactly the lockfile
# semantics). If the command fails, the conflict is RESTORED for manual resolution.
# Disabled together with lockfile auto-resolve: --no-lockfile-auto or
# FORK_FLOW_NO_LOCKFILE_AUTO=1.
#
# NOTE: these commands are project-controlled CODE run by port.sh — the same trust
# class as a registry entry's Verify command. Review rules like you review hooks.
#
# Examples (commented out — uncomment/adapt for your project):
# db/schema.rb      bundle exec rails db:migrate
# db/structure.sql  bundle exec rails db:migrate
